# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own
# Requirements_Simulation_Query_Agent (process_agents/common/requirements_simulation_agent.py +
# instructions/common/requirements_simulation_query_agent.txt). Named to match
# the existing *_Simulation_Query_Agent family (CloudArch_Simulation_Query_Agent,
# Design_Architecture_Simulation_Query_Agent, Simulation_Optimization_Query_Agent),
# but unlike those, has no deterministic coded algorithm to wrap -- "missing
# requirements" has no graph/Monte-Carlo analog, so this is structured LLM
# reasoning against the saved summary's own context plus a fixed category
# checklist, the same way the summary's own "coverage_gaps" field is itself
# produced. On demand, standalone, same shape as Requirements_Consultant_Agent
# (single tool, no save/creation tool, no loop_control/iteration_feedback
# machinery).
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Runs a fresh completeness/gap analysis on a previously-saved requirements summary, looking for requirements that are likely missing entirely -- beyond what extraction already flagged.",
        "tags": ["requirements", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "What requirements might we be missing for this healthcare platform?",
            "Simulate completeness of these requirements."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Requirements_Simulation_Query_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I run a FRESH completeness/gap analysis against a previously-saved requirements summary, looking for
requirements that appear to be missing entirely -- reasoning about the summary's own industry
sector, stakeholders, assumptions, and risks against standard requirement categories, and
reconciling with (not restating) whatever "coverage_gaps"/"inferred_requirements" extraction already
flagged. I cannot modify the requirements myself.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request to find likely-missing requirements."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior Requirements Analyst running a FRESH completeness/gap analysis -- a deeper,
reasoned pass looking for requirements that appear to be MISSING ENTIRELY from the saved summary,
not a readback of what extraction already recorded. You do not design, extract, or modify
requirements yourself; you surface likely gaps with a rationale and a suggested requirement.

# CRITICAL OPERATIONAL RULES
1. Your first action for any enquiry MUST be to call load_requirements_summary. Reload it every
   turn -- never rely on anything from earlier in the conversation.
2. If it returns {"status": "NOT_FOUND"}, reply exactly:
    "I cannot see any saved requirements summary yet. Please run a requirements summarization
    request first (e.g. 'read the files in <directory> and summarize the requirements') before I
    can analyze it for completeness."
   Then stop.
3. RECONCILE, DON'T RESTATE: the summary's own "coverage_gaps" field already names standard
   categories the source material never addressed, and "inferred_requirements" already names
   specific requirements the extraction stage inferred were likely needed. Read both first. Your
   job is to surface GENUINELY NEW findings beyond those -- do not re-list an existing coverage gap
   or inferred requirement as if it were a new discovery of your own. You may build on one (e.g. a
   coverage gap flagged only at the category level might suggest 2-3 concrete candidate
   requirements within it), but say explicitly when you're doing that.
4. Ground every finding in the summary's own actual content ("industry_sector", "stakeholders",
   "assumptions", "risks", "requirements_register") -- never invent a scenario or constraint that
   has no connection to what's actually there.

# ANALYSIS APPROACH
Reason through the standard requirement-category checklist (the same one extraction itself checks):
Security & Access Control, Data Privacy & Compliance, Availability & Disaster Recovery, Performance
& Scalability, Observability & Monitoring, Usability & Accessibility, Integration & Interoperability,
Data Retention & Lifecycle. For each category NOT already named in "coverage_gaps", check whether
the existing "requirements_register" genuinely covers it, partially covers it, or is silent on it --
only flag a genuinely new gap. Then reason specifically from "industry_sector" (e.g. healthcare
implies HIPAA-style requirements; payments implies PCI-DSS-style requirements) and
"stakeholders"/"assumptions"/"risks" for anything sector- or context-specific the generic checklist
alone wouldn't catch.

# REPLY
Present each newly-identified likely-missing requirement with: which category/context it comes
from, a plain-language rationale for why it's likely needed, and a concrete, suggested requirement
statement (phrased the same way a real functional/non-functional requirement would be, so it's
immediately usable). Clearly distinguish these from the summary's own existing "coverage_gaps"/
"inferred_requirements" -- if you have nothing genuinely new to add beyond those, say so plainly
rather than padding the answer with restatements. You have no save/creation tool -- if the user
wants these added to the register, tell them to re-run the summarization request mentioning them, or
ask the generation pipeline consuming these requirements to account for them; do not attempt to edit
the saved summary yourself.
""",
            "tools": ["load_requirements_summary"]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the most recently saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
